  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 AM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nVigorous shortwave just ne of Georgian Bay will accelerate\neastward, moving thru the base of a broader upper trof over\neastern Canada. Shortwave ridging builds into the northern lakes\ntoday, in advance of another digging shortwave that gives us a\nglancing blow tonight. As ridging in the western US/Canada\nbecomes more amplified this week, more energy drives southward\ninto the central/eastern Conus. This will deepen troffing in the\nGreat Lakes later this week, and allow a system to organize in\nthe east half of the country Wed-Thu. Much colder air is\nexpected here behind this system for Thu-Fri. \n\nSct lake effect rain showers continue early this morning, though\ncoverage/intensity have been decreasing. Expect precip to\ncontinue to wane early this morning, though clouds will be\nslower to erode. Clouds associated with our next shortwave trof\nwill return to the north late today, and a small chance for a\nshowers will be mentioned for eastern upper MI for part of this\nevening. \n\nOtherwise mainly quiet tonight into Tue night. However, pops\nincrease Wed-Thu. The primary surface low track is looking to \nbe well to our south, from southern IA to southern OH. But a \nsecondary e-w fgen zone, more poleward than the primary one, \nwill be working south across our region mid-week. Moisture and \nprecip chances will be increasing Wed/Wed night, especially in \nnorthern lower MI. This is also when colder air begins to push \nin, with potential lake enhanced precip thus a factor. However, \nthe expected ne to n fetch is not a favorable one, except for \nrelatively small spots in ne and nw lower MI. What precip we \nget will be mostly rain Wed/Wed night, but become snowier by the\nvery end of the week.\n\nAbove normal temps today and Tuesday. Sharply cooler for Thu-\nFri, with Wed a transition day.",
